// Per-developer signing, the fallback template. tools/config-local.sh writes
// Config/Local.xcconfig (gitignored) on the first build from the Apple Development
// identity in the keychain, and only copies this file when there is no such identity.
// Config/Base.xcconfig includes that copy if it exists. Edit the copy, never this
// template.
//
// The values below are ad-hoc signing, the same thing CI passes on the command line.
// They build and run every target on the machine that produced them, so a checkout on a
// machine with no developer identity still works without any setup.
//
// Ad-hoc signing gives the binary a new code signature on every build, so macOS treats
// each build as a new application and re-asks for the permissions the UI tests, the
// screenshot tooling, and reading the game install off an external volume need. Signing
// with a real Apple Development identity keeps those grants across builds. To do that,
// set both values below: the identity as `security find-identity -v -p codesigning`
// prints it (the literal string `Apple Development` also works), and your ten-character
// Team ID, which is the OU field of that certificate:
//
//   security find-certificate -c "Apple Development" -p | openssl x509 -noout -subject
//
//   OPENSKY_CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = Apple Development
//   OPENSKY_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = ABCDE12345

OPENSKY_CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = -
OPENSKY_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =
